s the country does not count as money), so the rows add up to broad money. Other items, net, is the survey's balancing line (provisions, payments on their way between banks, valuation differences), shown with the sign that makes the rows add up. Money and credit are two sides of the same balance sheet, so this is never added to a credit or debt figure. The IMF counts money the same way in every country, which can be wider than the country's own measure of money. The lines the IMF publishes for T\u00fcrkiye leave up to 0.18% of broad money unplaced in the last two years, shown as its own row. Source: International Monetary Fund."}